-- title:
--   Drinfeld local chart at a supersingular point, full level
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$ with $5 \le q$, a nonzero natural number $M'$ with $q \nmid M'$, and a prime $\ell$ with $3 \le \ell$, $\ell \ne q$ and $\ell \nmid M'$.
--
--   *Coefficient field and roots of unity.* $L$ is a field of characteristic zero which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ for the set $\{q\ell\}$, $\zeta \in L$ is a primitive $q$-th root of unity and $\xi \in L$ is a primitive $q\ell$-th root of unity.
--
--   *The function field.* $K$ is an intermediate field of the Laurent series field $L(\!(T)\!)$ over $L$, required by `hK` to equal [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103) applied to [`ModularCurve.xHFunctionField ((q*ℓ)^2*M') (ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH (q*ℓ) M')`](def/ModularCurve_XH.html#L79); that is, $K$ is the subfield of $L(\!(T)\!)$ generated over $L$ by the image, under the coefficientwise map [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81) induced by $\mathbb{Q} \to L$, of the $q$-expansion function field of level $N_0 = (q\ell)^2 M'$ and character group $H = \ker\bigl((\mathbb{Z}/N_0)^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/q\ell)^\times\bigr)$, the group of units congruent to $1$ modulo $q\ell$.
--
--   *The base ring.* $A$ is a discrete valuation domain which is an $L$-algebra-compatible subring of $L$ in the sense that $L$ is its fraction field, with $q$ lying in the maximal ideal of $A$ (`hAq`), with $\zeta$ in the image of $A \to L$ (`hζA`), with an $A$-algebra structure on $K$ compatible with $A \to L \to K$, and $\varpi \in A$ a generator of the maximal ideal (`hϖ`).
--
--   *The $j$-invariant and the model.* $j \in K$ is nonzero and has Laurent expansion [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81), the $q$-expansion $T^{-1}\cdot(\text{power series } j_{\mathrm{Num}})$ of the modular $j$-function (`hj`). The scheme in play is [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel A K j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L236), the pushout of the two charts attached to $j$ over $A$; its finite chart algebra `chartAlgFin A K j` is the $A$-subalgebra of $K$ of elements integral over $A[j]$, `XFin` is its spectrum, `jChartFin` is $j$ viewed inside that algebra, and `toBase` is the structure morphism to $\operatorname{Spec} A$.
--
--   *The point.* $z$ is a point of the model; $\varpi_z$ is the germ at $z$ of the global section obtained from $\varpi$ by $A \to \Gamma(\operatorname{Spec} A) \to \Gamma(\mathfrak{X})$ along `toBase` (`hϖz`), and `hz` requires $\varpi_z$ to lie in the maximal ideal of the stalk at $z$, so that $z$ lies in the special fibre. $y$ is a point of `XFin` with `ιFin` sending $y$ to $z$ (`hy`).
--
--   *Supersingularity (`hss`).* For every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi$ from `chartAlgFin A K j` to $\Omega$ whose kernel is the prime ideal $y$, the value $\varphi(j)$ lies in [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7), i.e. every elliptic Weierstrass curve over $\Omega$ with that $j$-invariant has only the zero point killed by $q$.
--
--   Under these hypotheses there exist: a type $W$ with a commutative ring structure making it a discrete valuation domain which is adically complete for its maximal ideal; a ring homomorphism $\sigma : A \to W$ with maximal ideal of $W$ generated by $\sigma\varpi$; power series $f, u, v \in W[\![X_0, X_1]\!]$ with $u$ and $v$ units and $f - (X_0X_1^{\,q} - X_0^{\,q}X_1) \in (X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$, the difference from [`DrinfeldCurve.LocalChart.drinfeldForm q W`](def/DrinfeldCurve_LocalChart.html#L18); and a ring isomorphism
--   $$e : \widehat{\mathcal{O}}_{\mathfrak{X},z} \;\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\; S := W[\![X_0,X_1]\!]/\bigl(C(\sigma\varpi)\,v - f\,u\bigr),$$
--   where the left-hand side is the adic completion of the stalk $\mathrm{STK}$ at $z$ with respect to its maximal ideal, written $\mathrm{CMP}$, with $\mathrm{toC} : \mathrm{STK} \to \mathrm{CMP}$ the canonical map, $\mathrm{mkS} : W[\![X_0,X_1]\!] \to S$ the quotient map, and $\mathrm{germY}$ the ring homomorphism from `chartAlgFin A K j` to the stalk obtained by passing through the affine chart `ιFin` and taking the germ at $z$; these data satisfy six conjuncts.
--
--   (1) *Constants.* For every $a \in A$, $e$ sends the image in $\mathrm{CMP}$ of the germ at $z$ of $a$ (transported along `toBase`) to the class of the constant series $C(\sigma a)$.
--
--   (2) *Stability of the chart algebra.* For every $\gamma \in \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ lying in $\Gamma_0(M')$ and every $L$-algebra automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ satisfying [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.IsLevelAutAt L (q*ℓ) ξ (q*ℓ) ((q*ℓ)^2*M') H γ⁻¹ K τ`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelLevelAutAt.html#L29) — i.e. $\tau$ realises, on ratios of modular forms for $\Gamma_H(N_0)$ with integral $q$-expansions, the action of the $\mathrm{diag}(q\ell,1)$-conjugate of $\gamma^{-1}$, after any complex embedding $\iota$ of $L$ carrying $\xi$ to $e^{2\pi i/(q\ell)}$ — the automorphism $\tau$ maps `chartAlgFin A K j` into itself.
--
--   (3) *Fixing the point $y$.* For every $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M') \cap \Gamma(\ell)$ and every $\tau$ as in (2) for $\gamma^{-1}$, and for any witness that $\tau$ preserves the chart algebra, the restriction of $\tau$ to the chart algebra satisfies $\tau(a) - a \in y$ for all $a$ in that algebra.
--
--   (4) *Linearisation of the level action.* For every $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$, every $\tau$ as in (2) for $\gamma^{-1}$, every witness that $\tau$ preserves the chart algebra, and on the assumption that $\tau(a) - a \in y$ for all $a$ in the chart algebra, there exist a ring automorphism $\theta$ of $S$, an element $c \in W$ and a matrix $M \in \mathrm{Mat}_{2\times 2}(W)$ such that: $\theta \circ e \circ \mathrm{toC} \circ \mathrm{germY} = e \circ \mathrm{toC} \circ \mathrm{germY} \circ \tau$ on the chart algebra; $\theta$ fixes every constant class $\mathrm{mkS}(C(w))$, $w \in W$; for each index $j$, $\theta(\mathrm{mkS}(X_j)) - \mathrm{mkS}\bigl(\sum_i C(M_{ij}) X_i\bigr)$ lies in the square of the ideal generated by $\mathrm{mkS}(X_0)$ and $\mathrm{mkS}(X_1)$; $c^{q+1} - 1$ lies in the maximal ideal of $W$; $M_{ij} \equiv c\,\gamma_{ij}$ modulo the maximal ideal of $W$ for all $i,j$; if $\gamma \in \Gamma(\ell)$ then $c \equiv 1$ modulo the maximal ideal; and if $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau$ is not the identity on $K$ then $c - 1$ does not lie in the maximal ideal.
--
--   (5) *Separation of branches.* For all integers $a_1, b_1, a_2, b_2$ and all prime ideals $P_1, P_2$ of $S$ such that neither $P_i$ contains both $\mathrm{mkS}(X_0)$ and $\mathrm{mkS}(X_1)$, both contain $\mathrm{mkS}(C(\sigma\varpi))$, and for each $i$ there is $h \in (X_0,X_1)^2$ with $\mathrm{mkS}\bigl(C(a_i)X_0 + C(b_i)X_1 + h\bigr) \in P_i$, if $q \nmid a_1b_2 - a_2b_1$ then the contractions of $P_1$ and $P_2$ along $\mathrm{toC}$ followed by $e$ are distinct ideals of the stalk.
--
--   (6) *Valuative description of one branch.* For every prime ideal $P$ of $S$ not containing both $\mathrm{mkS}(X_0)$ and $\mathrm{mkS}(X_1)$, containing $\mathrm{mkS}(C(\sigma\varpi))$, and for which there is $h \in (X_0,X_1)^2$ with $\mathrm{mkS}\bigl(C(1)X_0 + C(0)X_1 + h\bigr) \in P$, and for every $a$ in the chart algebra: $\mathrm{toC}(\mathrm{germY}(a))$ lies in the contraction of $P$ along $e$ if and only if, for every $n \in \mathbb{Z}$, the $n$-th Laurent coefficient of $a$ in $L(\!(T)\!)$ lies in the image of the maximal ideal of $A$ under $A \to L$.
--
--   This is the local study, at a supersingular point in residue characteristic $q$, of the two-chart integral model over a discrete valuation ring of the modular function field of level $\Gamma_H((q\ell)^2M')$ with $H$ the units congruent to $1$ modulo $q\ell$: the completed stalk is presented as Drinfeld's local chart $W[\![X_0,X_1]\!]/(\varpi v - fu)$ with $f \equiv X_0X_1^q - X_0^qX_1$ modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$, together with the linearised action of the level automorphisms, separation of the branches through the point, and a valuative characterisation of one branch. It is used in the form with an algebraically closed residue field and a Hasse-type moduli condition, on the way to the ramification and reduction statements for the auxiliary level structures.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_FullLevel_AuxLevel_exists_ringEquiv_adicCompletion_stalk_drinfeldChart_of_mem_ssJSet_twoChartIntegralModel.lean

